div><div><div>VOP2 fetches the scanout data for all of its video ports ove=
r a single</div>=0A<div>AXI clock.&nbsp; On RK3588 that clock is pinned to=
 500 MHz by the device tree</div>=0A<div>and nothing ever raises it, so a =
mode whose pixel rate outruns what the</div>=0A<div>AXI clock can deliver =
underruns the internal scanout FIFO.&nbsp; The hardware</div>=0A<div>repor=
ts this as POST_BUF_EMPTY and the picture is corrupted.</div>=0A<div>&nbsp=
;</div>=0A<div>3840x2160@120 over DisplayPort is such a mode.&nbsp; Measur=
ed on an Orange Pi</div>=0A<div>5 Plus by moving the rate at runtime while=
 the mode was up, with dclk</div>=0A<div>unchanged at 594 MHz throughout, =
so that the AXI rate was the only</div>=0A<div>variable:</div>=0A<div>&nbs=
p;</div>=0A<div>&nbsp; 500 MHz: ~594000 suppressed vop2_isr callbacks per =
5 s, corrupted</div>=0A<div>&nbsp; 750 MHz: no POST_BUF_EMPTY at all for t=
he 42 s the phase lasted, clean</div>=0A<div>&nbsp; 500 MHz: ~607000 suppr=
essed callbacks per 5 s, corrupted again</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;</div>=0A<div>=
Both transitions are immediate.&nbsp; Heiko Stuebner reports the same</div=
>=0A<div>starvation on different hardware [1].</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;</div>=
=0A<div>The requirement follows each port's pixel rate rather than its int=
erface</div>=0A<div>clock, and it is per port rather than aggregate.&nbsp;=
 Two measurements at</div>=0A<div>500 MHz pin that down:</div>=0A<div>&nbs=
p;</div>=0A<div>&nbsp; - a single port scanning out 3840x2160@120 underrun=
s, while the same</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; composed pixel rate spre=
ad over three ports - 3840x2160@60 on one</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; =
and 3840x2160@30 on two others - is clean for a minute with no</div>=0A<di=
v>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; underrun on any of them.&nbsp; The totals are equal t=
o the pixel:</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 3840*2160*120 =3D=3D 3840*216=
0*(60+30+30).</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;</div>=0A<div>&nbsp; - 3840x2160@60 is cl=
ean where 3840x2160@120 is not, although both run</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp; dclk at 594 MHz on this board: the 120 Hz link is YCbCr 4:2:0, whi=
ch</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; halves dclk without halving the rate at=
 which the port consumes</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; pixels.</div>=0A<=
div>&nbsp;</div>=0A<div>So the condition belongs on each video port's own =
crtc_clock.&nbsp; Summing</div>=0A<div>across ports would be wrong, and ke=
ying on dclk would miss 4:2:0</div>=0A<div>entirely.&nbsp; The threshold s=
its between the measured points: 3840x2160@60</div>=0A<div>(594000 kHz) an=
d 2560x1440@144 (about 586000 kHz) are both clean at the</div>=0A<div>defa=
ult rate, 3840x2160@120 (1188000 kHz) is not.</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;</div>=0A=
<div>Track the requirement as a global atomic state object rather than by<=
/div>=0A<div>walking the CRTC list when the rate is applied.&nbsp; Each CR=
TC records what</div>=0A<div>it needs during its own atomic check, and the=
 rate applied is the</div>=0A<div>maximum over the ports.&nbsp; Going thro=
ugh the atomic state is what makes</div>=0A<div>this safe: a commit only e=
ver writes the entry for a CRTC it holds a</div>=0A<div>lock for, and neve=
r reads the state of a CRTC that a concurrent commit</div>=0A<div>may be s=
wapping underneath it.&nbsp; The rate the platform set up is used as</div>=
=0A<div>the lower bound, so a board that already configures a higher rate =
keeps</div>=0A<div>it.</div>=0A<div>&nbsp;</div>=0A<div>Tested on the same=
 board on drm-misc-next plus the dw-dp and Rockchip</div>=0A<div>USBDP PHY=
